#### 4.2 AgentFacts Standard Implementation

The AgentFacts standard provides a structured way to represent AI Agent metadata. Here's an example of a complete AgentFacts JSON structure that would be stored in the NFT metadata:

```json
{
  "id": "did:web:agent.ai:agents:research-assistant-v1",
  "agent_name": "urn:agent:agent:research-assistant",
  "facts_url": "https://agent.ai/agents/research-assistant/.agent-facts",
  "private_facts_url": "https://agentfacts.nanda.ai/agent/research-assistant/private",
  "adaptive_router_url": "https://resolver.agent.com/capabilities",
  "ttl": 3600,
  "signature": "eyJhbGciOiJFZERTQSIsInR5cCI6IkpXVCJ9...",
  "capabilities": {
    "modalities": ["text", "structured_data"],
    "skills": ["analysis", "synthesis", "research"]
  },
  "endpoints": {
    "static": ["https://api.agent.ai/v1/agents/research-assistant"],
    "adaptive_resolver": {
      "url": "https://resolver.agent.com/capabilities",
      "policies": ["capability_negotiation", "load_balancing"]
    }
  },
  "certification": {
    "level": "verified",
    "issuer": "NANDA",
    "attestations": ["privacy_compliant", "security_audited"]
  }
}
```

The agent URI is used as the adaptive router URL, linking to the executable file for the actual agent. This structure allows clients to:

1. Discover agent capabilities through standardized metadata
2. Verify the authenticity of the agent through cryptographic signatures
3. Access the agent through both static endpoints and dynamic routing services
4. Cache agent information for specified periods to reduce lookup overhead
5. Validate certifications and attestations from trusted authorities

When creating an Agent NFT, the metadata will follow this structure, ensuring compatibility with other systems that implement the AgentFacts standard.

